{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310814,"nonce":"cEMzrBUOn+umf971cCC6c6P6FY4=","title":"NEON-SIMD optimisations on rayzor_tensor_topk_scan landed at commit 3730292. Recent-ids contains() walks 8 i64s per iter via vceqq_s64; ...","message":"Measured on 80-token \"Write a short poem about the sea\" decode\n(M1 Pro, llama-chat, llvm-backend build, RAYZOR_KERNEL_TIMING=1):\n\n|                  | Before                | After                 | Delta |\n|------------------|----------------------|----------------------|-------|\n| topk_scan/call   | 2848 us              | 1043 us              | **2.73x faster** |\n| topk_scan share  | 9.8% of decode       | 3.8% of decode       | -6.0pp |\n| Total tok/s      | 32.85                | 34.92                | **+6.3%** |\n| Stability (5 runs) | -                  | 33.4-35.3 tok/s      | clean |\n\nLong-form 300-token validated: 1042 us/call (consistent).\n\n\nTwo NEON-SIMD additions in `rayzor_tensor_topk_scan`\n([runtime/src/tensor.rs](runtime/src/tensor.rs#L1332)):\n\n1. **recent_contains()** — replaces the inner `for \u0026r in recent {if r ==\n   target ...}` linear walk with a chunked vceqq_s64 + horizontal-OR\n   pass over 8 i64s per iteration. Hits the chat workload's\n   `repetition_penalty = 1.15, recent_len = 64` path. Most candidates\n   miss, so the fast-reject path is the hot case; SIMD reduces it from\n   64 cmp+branch to ~8 vceqq + 1 horizontal OR.\n\n2. **Steady-state cutoff fast-reject** — load 4 f32 logits via\n   vld1q_f32, widen to 2x f64 via vcvt_f64_f32 + vcvt_high_f64_f32,\n   compare against the current cutoff via vcgtq_f64, horizontal-OR the\n   masks to detect if ANY of the 4 candidates beat the cutoff. In\n   steady-state the hit rate is ~0.5% (50/128k), so the ~99.5% reject\n   path is replaced by a single SIMD compare per chunk-of-4.\n\nThe penalize path still chunked-scalars the per-lane recent_contains\nlookup (can't easily SIMD-fuse with cutoff), but the 4-f32 vector load\n+ widening still amortises across 4 candidates.\n\n\nInsertion-sort tie-breaking is unchanged (`\u003c` not `\u003c=`, preserving the\nlower-index winner for ties), so the surviving top-K matches the prior\nscalar pass byte-identically. Paris MATCH preserved; 128/128 haxe\nregression green.\n\n\nPost-SIMD-topk breakdown (80-token decode):\n\n  matmul_qt_t_f32_threaded    1979 ms  (89.7%)\n  matmul_qkv_qt_t              90 ms  ( 4.1%)\n  topk_scan                    85 ms  ( 3.8%)\n  flash_attn_decode            51 ms  ( 2.3%)\n\nNow nearly 90% of decode is in `matmul_qt_t_f32_threaded` (the per-row\nQ4_K_M matmul), and from [[bugs-q4km-4block-paired-no-win]] we know\nthe 2-block kernel already hits M1's ILP saturation point. Next\nrealistic levers there are at the scheduling layer (thread affinity to\nP-cores) or a higher-level redesign (2x2 output tile per the original\nquant.rs:2591 note).\n\n\n- [[perf-kernel-breakdown-2026-06-07]] — the breakdown that pinpointed\n  topk_scan as worth optimising.\n- [[perf-ffi-batching-topk]] — earlier FFI-batching win that gave\n  +23% short-form by folding the 128k-iter scan into one FFI call.\n  This new SIMD win stacks on top of that.\n- [[bugs-q4km-4block-paired-no-win]] — the saturation point on the now\n  90%-dominant matmul kernel.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_topk_scan_neon_simd` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}